\par \pard\plain \s3\hich\af6\loch\widctlpar\sb240\sa60\keepn\f6\b\ql\ltrpar{\loch
Detailed Description}
\par \pard\plain \s62\loch\qj\widctlpar\sb30\sa60\fs22\ltrpar{\loch
Multiply matrices. }
\par \pard\plain \s62\loch\qj\widctlpar\sb30\sa60\fs22\ltrpar\loch

\par \pard\plain \s5\hich\af6\loch\widctlpar\sb90\sa30\keepn\f6\fs16\b\qj\ltrpar{\loch
Author}
\par \pard\plain \s62\loch\qj\widctlpar\sb30\sa60\fs22\ql\fi0\li360\lin360\ltrpar{\loch\fs20\loch
Jack Wiegman}
\par \pard\plain \s62\loch\qj\widctlpar\sb30\sa60\fs22\ltrpar{\loch
Prompts user for matrix dimensions where the dimensions are between 1 and }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#AAAAAAAAAJ" }{\fldrslt {\loch\hich\af4\loch\cf2\f4\ul\ulc0\cf2\ul\ulc0\loch
MAX_MATRIX_DIMENSION}{}}}\loch
. Prompt for first matrix (M1) rows and columns, then build and print matrix, exiting if the user inputs a sentinel value. Build the matrix with random values from 0 to }{{\field{\*\fldinst HYPERLINK "#AAAAAAAAAI" }{\fldrslt {\loch\hich\af4\loch\cf2\f4\ul\ulc0\cf2\ul\ulc0\loch
MAX_MATRIX_VALUE}{}}}\loch
. So the matrices can be multiplied, set the column dimensions of the second matrix (M2) to the row dimensions of the first, then prompt for row dimensions for the second matrix and build then print it.}
\par \pard\plain \s62\loch\qj\widctlpar\sb30\sa60\fs22\ltrpar{\loch
Multiply the matrices by obtaining the dot product of each row and column for the matrices. Take the Nth row of M1 and the Mth column of M2. These have the same number of elements, so take the product of M1[n][1] and M2[1][m] summed to M1[n][2] * M2[2][m] and so on for all the elements in the row/column. This sum will be placed in the resulting matrix (M3) at the element M3[n][m].}
\par \pard\plain \s62\loch\qj\widctlpar\sb30\sa60\fs22\ltrpar{\loch
The program will then print the resulting product matrix. The loop will then end, and restart prompting the user for a matrix dimension. The user will continue to multiply matrices until they type -1, when prompted, to exit. }
